    "body": "The read-only BDB parser in migrate.cpp runs on any file passed to `migratewallet`\r\n(RPC or GUI) or `bitcoin-wallet`. #34959 added checks for btree levels and overflow\r\nlengths, which stop simple cycles, but a crafted file can still make the parser hang\r\nor use a lot of memory:\r\n\r\n- A btree page can be the child of more than one internal page. Its level still\r\n  checks out, so it gets parsed again for every path that reaches it. On master an\r\n  ~8 KB file built this way doesn't finish.\r\n- An overflow page with no data doesn't count toward the length #34959 checks, so\r\n  two such pages pointing at each other loop forever. Separately, any number of\r\n  records can point at the same overflow chain, and each one reads and stores its\r\n  own copy.\r\n- All of a page's index entries can point at the same record, which then gets read\r\n  and kept once per entry. A single 64 KB page can turn into ~500 MB this way. On\r\n  master a 256 KB file took `migratewallet` from 54 MB to ~800 MB.\r\n\r\nThe first two are fixed by tracking visited pages (the approach from #34946): btree\r\npages in one set, and overflow pages in another that covers the whole tree. For the\r\nthird, a page is rejected once its records add up to more than the page size.\r\n\r\nThis shouldn't affect real wallets. In a valid btree every page has a single parent\r\nand every overflow page belongs to a single record. Records on a page can't overlap\r\neither, since the parser already rejects databases that allow duplicates.\r\n\r\nEach fix comes with a test in db_tests.cpp that builds the bad file by hand, plus a\r\ntest that a page filled to the last byte still parses. `wallet_migration.py` passes\r\nwith v28.2.\r\n\r\nThe last commit adds two errors from #34959 to the `wallet_bdb_parser` fuzz target.\r\nThey were missing, so the target aborted on inputs that reach them. I ran into this\r\nwhile feeding mutated copies of the test files through the target.\r\n\r\nSupersedes #35992.\r\n",
